From 43e5185634f4abbd84b477e463df9e9e205cbbfd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 08:08:39 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 11/16] [drop] release integer members in place and
 tail-release the last child

A record that holds an arbitrary-precision integer reports an observable
cleanup, because every member type that is not trivially copyable does.
The worklist glue asks that flag per record in two places.
`dispatchLeaf` refuses the record, so a dead value released from user
code opens a destruction scope even when its arm holds only integers.
`releasePure` is off, so every dead child is deferred and popped again,
with no tail call. But an integer release runs no cleanup hook; the
tail-loop drop already releases integers in place.

The glue now asks the members (`classify`): a `Leaf` holds scalars and
integers, `Boxes` adds shared boxes whose release can wait, anything
else (a foreign object, a closure) is `Ordered`. An arm that is a leaf
is released where it is found, its integers in place. Under backward
order `releasePure` serves every arm that is not `Ordered`, also when a
cleanup hook is in reach behind a box; an `Ordered` arm keeps the
ordered release. The order of the hooks does not change: the backward
walk also decrements every box at its visit and defers the dead ones,
and the box that `releasePure` continues in is the one the work list
would pop next. The forward walk decrements a box only when its turn
comes, which a shared box can observe, so under forward order
`releasePure` stays limited to records with no observable cleanup: only
the leaf dispatch is new there.

Measured with a prototype that generates the same LLVM IR as this form
for the six programs (compared function by function): wall time against
the unpatched compiler, median of 9, 0.846 and 0.839 (symbolic
derivative, two sizes), 0.846 and 0.875 (red-black tree with integer
keys), 0.933, 0.958; six control programs 0.988 to 1.003. On the
derivative program the destruction scopes entered fall from 565,495 to 1
and the deferred releases from 2,426,392 to 652,241; on the tree program
the deferred releases fall from 1,053,560 to 204,963. Call sites of
`__reussir_drop_run` in the optimized IR: 94 to 31, 97 to 34, 15 to 6.
Of the benchmark suite, core and std (33 units) three change:
bigint-rbtree, nbe-hoas and std gain tail calls in their release
helpers and lose deferrals; the lowered MLIR of the others is identical.

The cost is code size, in the release helpers and where user code
releases a leaf arm in place: the optimized IR of a derivative-style
program grows by 3.8% and that of two large programs by 0.3% and 0.2%,
the object of one unit of the benchmark suite by 4.4%, and that of a
synthetic type with 19 arms by 39% at the aggressive level.

Tests: conversion/worklist_integer_members.mlir (a leaf arm of an
integer, an arm with a foreign object, an arm of boxes and an integer,
both drop orders), conversion/worklist_pure_order.mlir (hooks behind
the boxes of a pure record run in the order of the walk under both drop
orders, with a shared box; a chain of 100,000 needs no stack).
